# sync_timedb hot-path janitor lock priority

**Activation:** `alwaysApply: false` — when triggers in `agent-discipline-core.mdc` match, **Read this file before editing**. Triggered rules are mandatory, not optional.

**Law:** ingest/populate/append is the **hot path**; janitor seal/dedupe/delete/tar-drop is the **cold path**. Janitor **defers before write** and **cooperatively yields mid-mutation** when ingest signals need the same daily tar after a janitor-first lock — **except daily tar restore from sealed** (missing or corrupt `.tar`), where restore runs to completion under an **exclusive owner-token lease** (`SET NX EX` + compare-and-del clear) and populate **waits** on `daily_tar_restore`. **Non-owner clears are forbidden** (loser must not `DELETE` the lease). **Day-close is single-flight per tar** across ticks (live-worker tars excluded from pop/enqueue). A day-close worker that loses the restore race **defers** (`reason=daily_tar_restore`) instead of blocking a pool slot for the full decompress. **Archive append dispatch** must still **skip** restore-blocked calendar days (overflow + short backoff) so free archive-pool slots drain other heap days; restore ownership stays on the decompress owner thread.

**Operator signature of the dual-write bug:** two concurrent `zstd -d -o …/{day}.tar.decomp.tmp` processes (materialize-to-tar). That is **not** Redis populate — populate uses `zstd -d -c` (stdout pipe) under the populate lock.

